        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<br>A number of physiological changes have been demonstrated in bone, muscle and blood from exposure of humans and animals to microgravity.  It was hypothesized that during space flight, all of the following would increase:  atrophy of the muscles, the intervertebral disc space, and percentage of fat in the bone marrow.  It was also hypothesized that the changes in bone marrow fat would be accompanied by a decrease in the cellular component.  Determining the mechanisms of space-flight-induced physiological changes and the development of effective countermeasures for long duration space missions is an important goal of the National Aeronautics and Space Administration (NASA).  
<P>
Magnetic Resonance Imaging (MRI) scans performed on the leg and lower back muscles of the crewmembers of the Spacelab-J (STS-47) mission showed significant muscle atrophy after only eight days in microgravity.  The 17-day Life and Microgravity Spacelab (LMS) mission provided the opportunity to determine whether long duration space flight would result in postflight changes and whether measurements taken early after landing would demonstrate any expansion of intervertebral discs.<P>The specific objectives of this investigation were:<br>
1. To measure the volumes of the calf, thigh, and back muscles using Magnetic Resonance Imaging (MRI) and to measure whole body lean mass using Dual Energy X-ray Absorptiometry (DEXA) before and after flight.  These data were compared with pre- and postflight muscle performance measurements.
<br>
2. To determine the change in intervertebral disc size and spine length in the lumbar spine after flight and determine the rate of recovery after return to 1-G.
<br>
3. To determine the changes in the muscle volumes (by MRI) and soft tissue distribution (by DEXA) between landing day and two days after landing, and to determine the calf muscle transverse relaxation time (T2) before and after flight.
<br>
4. To determine the bone marrow transverse relaxation time (T2) and cellularity of the Lumbar 3 (L3) vertebra before and after flight using MRI spectroscopy.<P>
APPROACH:<BR>
Magnetic Resonance Imaging (MRI) was used to measure changes in the volume of individual muscles (soleus, gastrocnemius, quadriceps, hamstrings, adductors, intrinsic low back, and psoas) and to determine the degree and rate of recovery to their preflight states.  During all MRI sessions, measurements were made in the same order, starting with the spine and back, and followed by the limb measurements.  <P>For spine imaging, subjects were positioned so that the imaging area was centered within the L3 vertebra.  The cross sectional area of the disc space was measured for the T12-L1, L1-2, L2-3, L3-4, and L4-5 discs.  For imaging of the back muscles, twenty (20)  0.5 cm slices, centered on the L3 vertebra, were obtained.  For limb measurements, the feet were positioned in a holder to minimize movement during image acquisition and to ensure repositioning.  For the thigh and calf muscles, thirty-two (32) 1 cm slices were taken.<P>The calf muscle transverse relaxation time (T2) was measured by repeating the calf region scan using the same receiver gain with a longer echo time.  The T2 values of each pixel were determined by comparing the image intensity at two different echo times, Te=10 and 50 msec.  The determined T2 value represents the averages of the individual pixels taken from each muscle.<P>To measure fat and water percentages in the vertebral bone marrow, volume selective proton spectra were obtained using a surface receive coil.  A cubic volume of interest of 15mm x 15mm x 15mm, located at the center of the L3 vertebra, was selected from the scout scan.  The spectra were obtained at four echo times (Te = 12,18, 24, 30 msec).   Bone marrow cellularity was determined from the fat to water peak areas.  <P>At the end of each MRI session, a whole body Dual Energy X-ray Absorptiometry (DEXA) scan was performed as well.  The DEXA scan required approximately 15 minutes and gave a radiation exposure of less than 1 mrem.  The whole body scan was analyzed for bone mineral density (BMD), soft tissue, lean tissue, and fat.  In order to examine fluid redistribution between the upper body and the lower limbs, the soft tissue values of the legs were divided by the whole body soft tissue minus the legs.  This ratio was used to compare preflight status with postflight status.<P>MRI and DEXA scans were performed on four crewmembers two times preflight, at 50-51 and 29-31 days before launch.  The scans were repeated postflight within 24 hours of Shuttle landing (R+0), and 2, 10, and 30 days after landing (R+2, R+10, R+30).<P>
RESULTS:<BR>
Muscle volume:  There were no significant changes in the volume of the psoas, anterior calf and gastrocnemius measured immediately after return to Earth (R+0).  Significant decreases of 3 to 10% were measured in the volume of soleus, quadriceps, hamstrings and intrinsic back muscles.  The data were compared with the measurements obtained on R+1 on Spacelab-J (8-day mission). By comparison, the R+0 muscle volume changes after LMS were somewhat greater, while the R+2 LMS data are somewhat less than the Spacelab-J data obtained on R+1.  <P>The second measurement taken at 10 days after landing of the LMS mission revealed that no differences in muscle volume were apparent when compared to preflight baseline.  These results were different than the previous results from Spacelab-J, which showed some muscle deficit remaining two weeks after landing (R+14 days). <P>Fluid redistribution:  The muscle volume data measured at R+0 and R+2 days clearly indicated that fluids moved into the lower limbs after flight.  One or two days after landing, most muscles regained a volume in the range of 3 to 6 % (compared to R+0 data).  There were two possible explanations for this phenomenon: (1) the muscles were dehydrated during weightlessness and the fluids were redistributed from the upper body because of the gravity vector, or (2) the fluid accumulation was caused by postflight muscle damage and ongoing muscle repair.  The first possible explanation was investigated by examining the soft tissue ratio of the legs to upper body from the DEXA scan before and after flight.  There was no significant change in the preflight and postflight ratios, indicating that the inflight fluid shift to the upper body from the legs was already reverted back to baseline by the time measurements were taken on R+0. This might indicate that the gravity dependent fluid shift is not responsible for the muscle swelling seen postflight.<P>To investigate the second theory, the T2 of the calf muscles was measured.  The T2 is sensitive to changes in tissue water/protein content.  The R+2 and R+10, but not the R+30 data, for the anterior calf and soleus were significantly above baseline. The T2 of the gastrocnemius also appeared elevated, but did not reach significance, p=0.07.  These results appear to show an elevated T2 at R+2 and sometimes at both R+2 and R+10 which returns toward baseline by R+30.  The T2 is elevated at R+10 when muscle volume differences are not apparent, but performance decrements are still apparent (Narici). This finding suggests that reambulation after flight causes swelling of muscles that lasts several weeks and is probably associated with muscle damage and/or repair. <P>Intervertebral disc:  No evidence of disc expansion was measured on R+0.  A slight, but not significant decrease in disc area was observed postflight.  Also, the data showed a lengthening of the spine relative to baseline on R+0, which appears to be at variance with the disc area measurements.<P>Bone marrow:  The analysis of the proton spectroscopy data (T2 water, T2 fat, and percent water) from two preflight and seven postflight measurements showed no significant change in the fraction of the water (cellular) component immediately postflight.  However, subsequent postflight measurements may have indicated some change.  Immediately postflight, a decrease relative to baseline in the T2 of the cellular component was evident. Subsequent measurements showed that the T2 increased.  At R+30, an increase in T2 above baseline was clearly evident in all 4 crewmembers.  For 3 of the 4 participating crewmembers, T2 remained elevated above baseline for more than 4 months after landing.<P>Body composition:  The bone mineral density (BMD) did not change postflight.  Subregional analysis showed no significant change in the lumbar spine or pelvis BMD.  There were no significant changes in lean tissue after flight, but there appeared to be loss in total body fat (2.8kg), which paralleled changes in total body weight. <END>
